body{
  display: block;
  font-family: "Work Sans", sans-serif;
  font-size: 17px;
  text-align: center;
  text-align: -webkit-center;
  justify-content: center;
  padding: 0px;
  margin: 0px;
}

h2 {
  margin-top: 15px;
  color: #383838;
}

.container {
  max-width: 680px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  display: block;
}

.head-logo {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}

.text-block {
  margin-top: 20px;
  color: #9ca0a8;
}

.subject-block {
  display: block;
  /* 88%, not the 78% this carried before box-sizing came in. border-box pulls the
     2x10% padding inside the declared width, so the same number now describes the
     whole card instead of just its text column - the card lost 136px of the 680
     without anyone changing a width. 88% lands between the old 98% and that. */
  width: 88%;
  margin: 20px 1% 0px 1%;
  padding: 5px 10% 5px 10%;
  line-height: 26px;
  border-radius: 16px;
  /* The card needs an edge that survives dark mode, and the fill cannot provide it.
     #f5f5f5 sits ten steps from white - visible on a light page; a dark-mode client
     inverts it to ten steps from black, where the eye no longer separates it from
     the page. Raising the contrast only moves the cost to light mode: a single
     value cannot serve both ends.
     A mid-grey line can, and it sidesteps the question rather than answering it.
     Under RGB inversion 128 becomes 127 - one step, which no eye resolves. So the
     line looks the same whether a client leaves border colours alone or inverts
     them, and there is nothing left to find out. Mid-grey also carries against
     both grounds: roughly 3.9:1 on white, 4.9:1 on near-black.
     box-sizing keeps the two border pixels inside the declared width instead of
     adding to it, so the card cannot overflow a narrow window. */
  box-sizing: border-box;
  border: 1px solid #808080;
  background-color: #f5f5f5;
  text-align: left;
  font-weight: bold;
  color: #383838;
}

.memo-block{
  display: block;
  /* 88%, not the 78% this carried before box-sizing came in. border-box pulls the
     2x10% padding inside the declared width, so the same number now describes the
     whole card instead of just its text column - the card lost 136px of the 680
     without anyone changing a width. 88% lands between the old 98% and that. */
  width: 88%;
  margin: 10px 1% 40px 1%;
  padding: 20px 10% 20px 10%;
  border-radius: 16px;
  /* mid-grey edge, one step from its own inverse - see .subject-block above */
  box-sizing: border-box;
  border: 1px solid #808080;
  background-color: #f5f5f5;
  text-align: left;
  color: #696c72;
}


.content {
  display: block;
  /* 88%, not the 78% this carried before box-sizing came in. border-box pulls the
     2x10% padding inside the declared width, so the same number now describes the
     whole card instead of just its text column - the card lost 136px of the 680
     without anyone changing a width. 88% lands between the old 98% and that. */
  width: 88%;
  margin: 40px 1% 40px 1%;
  padding: 20px 10% 40px 10%;
  border-radius: 24px;
  /* mid-grey edge, one step from its own inverse - see .subject-block above */
  box-sizing: border-box;
  border: 1px solid #808080;
  background-color: #f5f5f5;
}

/* Text a person typed, wherever it appears - a memo, a moderator's question, the
   subject and body of a message. Html collapses the line breaks they pressed; this
   keeps them.

   ⛔ On the text itself, never on a block that holds other elements. Pug pretty-prints,
   so a container carries newlines of its OWN between its children, and `pre` rules make
   those visible too - a stray blank line in every mail. Inside `span.human-text= memo`
   there is nothing but the text.

   `pre-line` rather than `pre-wrap`, matching how the wallet shows the same messages
   (`ParseMessage.vue`): line breaks kept, runs of spaces collapsed, long lines still
   wrap. Nothing here is preformatted; it is prose somebody typed.

   ⚠️ Word-based clients (Outlook on Windows) support `white-space` poorly, so there the
   breaks may still collapse. Unmeasured - nobody here has Outlook. The alternative is
   emitting <br> per line, which every client renders; it is a bigger change and it
   leaves the way the rest of the software does this. Worth revisiting if it is reported. */
.human-text {
  white-space: pre-line;
}

.p_content{
  margin: 15px 0 15px 0;
  line-height: 26px;
  color: #696c72;
}

.clink {
  line-break: anywhere;
  margin-bottom: 40px;
}

/* Three buttons, three meanings, and they are the whole vocabulary of these mails:
   gold (.button-3) is the way on to wherever the thing lives, green (.button-5) is
   an answer to a person and therefore stands INSIDE the card, grey (.button-4) is
   the fallback ("new link"), red (.button-6) takes something away for good. */
.button-3,
.button-4,
.button-5,
.button-6 {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 9px 15px;
  color: white;
  border: 0;
  line-height: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  border-radius: 20px;
  background-image: radial-gradient(circle farthest-corner at 0% 0%, #f9cd69, #c58d38);
  box-shadow: 16px 13px 35px 0 rgba(56, 56, 56, 0.3);
  margin: 25px 0 25px 0;
  width: 50%;
}

.button-4 {
  background-image: radial-gradient(circle farthest-corner at 0% 0%, #616161, #c2c2c2);
}

.button-5 {
  /* background-image, not the background shorthand. The shorthand also sets
     background-color to transparent, and a dark-mode client reads that as "no
     background of its own" and paints its own over the gradient - which is why
     this button turned black while .button-3 above, written the same way but
     with background-image, kept its gold. */
  background-image: linear-gradient(135deg, #53900c, #6e6e6e);
  font-size: 20px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: #f5f5f5;
  width: auto;
  /* No shadow at all, not even the one inherited from the rule above. That one is
     rgba(56, 56, 56, 0.3) - a dark grey, which a dark-mode client inverts into a
     light halo around the button. Removing the button's own shadow made it inherit
     that one, so it has to be turned off explicitly. */
  box-shadow: none;
  transition: all 0.3s ease;
}

.button-6 {
  /* The only red in the mails. This button blocks a thank-you card, which cannot be
     taken back from the mail - it must not read as a sibling of the gold way-on
     button standing above it. Same shape as .button-3, only the hue differs, so it
     stays where a gold button stood.

     #f5365c is the wallet's own danger colour, read out of the shipped stylesheet
     (`.btn-danger{--bs-btn-bg:#f5365c}`) - the same red the confirmation dialogue wears,
     so the mail and the question it leads to are recognisably one thing.

     A gradient, not a flat fill: a dark-mode client inverts `background-color` and would
     turn a signal red into turquoise. It cannot invert `background-image`, which is why
     gold and green are gradients too. The second stop is the same red a shade deeper, so
     it still reads as one colour.

     White lettering, like every other button here: 3.77:1 against #f5365c - the same
     league as the green button at 3.92:1. The wallet uses black on this red (5.56:1)
     because Bootstrap computes it; a mail button with black lettering would be the odd
     one out among the other four. */
  background-image: linear-gradient(135deg, #f5365c, #d0264a);
  /* No shadow, for the reason given at .button-5: a dark-mode client turns the
     inherited dark grey shadow into a light halo around the button. */
  box-shadow: none;
}

.button-5:hover {
  transform: translateY(-5px);
  box-shadow: 20px 25px 30px rgba(56, 56, 56, 0.4);
}

